> What does it mean?

I think that's an open issue. "ke'a" here stands for a mass, and it
is not completelly agreed, as far as I know, what does a number in
front of a mass do. My preference is that it demassifies the mass
and refers to individual components. If you don't agree with that,
then you can use "pa lu'a ke'a" instead, which gives you one of the
elements of ke'a, i.e. one of the pair in this case.

